From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 09:56:41 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: add the AVM FRITZ!Box 7590 AX

The FRITZ!Box 7590 AX is AVM hardware revision HW259, built on the same
GRX550 die as the FRITZ!Box 7590. Its vendor tree is the 7590's in all
but the radios. hw259.dtsi includes the shared GRX550 layer and the
7590's leds-hw226.dtsi unchanged, so this file starts from the 7590's.
The switch wiring, USB, the front-panel lamps, the buttons and the LED
enable stay as they are there. The physical memory map is the 7590's,
so the memory node and its reservations stay too. The board carries an
FPGA, a Xilinx part on its public parts list, and grx550_fpga.dtsi is
the vendor's layer for it.

Five things differ from the 7590.

The model and compatible name the new board.

The sub-revision tag is 0, the key the vendor's Fritz_Box_HW259.dts
gives this board. It covers the units on record, which report 1 and 2.
The boot image carries a table of device-tree blobs keyed by hardware
sub-revision, and the boot loader looks the unit's value up in it or
takes a lower entry.

The TFFS is 8 MiB rather than 4 MiB. Both kernel slots and the UBI
partition therefore sit 4 MiB higher, and UBI shrinks to 487 MiB. The
flash is a Toshiba 512 MiB part with a 4 KiB page, as on the 7590. The
map and the memory size come from a public environment sample of one
unit.

The PCIe comment names this board's parts. RC0 and RC1 carry WAV600
Wi-Fi parts, and RC2 carries a DSL part of the VRX618 family. None of
them has an in-tree driver. The vendor's grx_common.dtsi gives RC2 the
7590's byte-swap and reset values, and hw259.dtsi does not override
them.

The front-panel controller changes in one place. The update rate is
10 Hz, for the reason given in commit 88adfeba8894 ("MIPS: dts:
intel-mips: add the AVM FRITZ!Box 7580"). The die layer's 250 kHz
selects a timer outside the SSO block that nothing in this kernel
programs. 10 Hz is the fastest rate that selects the block's own divided
clock. That choice is this kernel's and not the vendor's. The shift
clock and the ambient-brightness carrier on pin 0 stay as on the 7590,
the values of grx550_common.dtsi and leds-hw226.dtsi.

The comments carried over from the 7590 say the same things in plainer
sentences. Two claims are dropped. The boot-loader partition's comment
no longer says that it holds the only copy of the Wi-Fi calibration
data, since that is unverified on this board. The sub-revision comment
no longer calls the tag cosmetic, since the boot loader selects a table
entry by it.

No unit is available, and none has booted this tree.

Sources: hw259.dtsi, leds-hw226.dtsi, grx550_common.dtsi,
grx550_fpga.dtsi, grx_common.dtsi and Fritz_Box_HW259.dts under
arch/mips/boot/dts/lantiq/avm/ in the vendor's 08.25 GPL release for
the FRITZ!Box 7590 AX. The flash map and the memory size rest on one
full environment sample of a unit on boxmatrix.info, page "FRITZ!Box
7590 AX", fetched 2026-10-24. The sub-revisions rest on that sample,
which reads 2, and on two more units there, which read 1. The parts
list comes from the same page.

Link: https://boxmatrix.info/wiki/FRITZ!Box_7590_AX
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile
index 93b0e070e79e..6c9a0afbd238 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile
@@ -3,3 +3,4 @@ dtb-y	+= easy350_anywan.dtb
 dtb-y	+= seale_avm_fritz7560.dtb
 dtb-y	+= seale_avm_fritz7580.dtb
 dtb-y	+= seale_avm_fritz7590.dtb
+dtb-y	+= seale_avm_fritz7590ax.dtb
diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590ax.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590ax.dts
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..349bc91c3dda
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590ax.dts
@@ -0,0 +1,356 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+// Copyright (C) 2026 Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+/*
+ * The AVM FRITZ!Box 7590 AX is hardware revision HW259 on the GRX550 die
+ * for HWSubRevision 0, 1 and 2.
+ * This file holds only its board-specific data. The SoC is described in
+ * xrx500.dtsi, the board family in seale_avm.dtsi and the die variant in
+ * seale_avm_grx550.dtsi.
+ */
+/dts-v1/;
+
+#include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/input/input.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/leds/common.h>
+
+#include "seale_avm_grx550.dtsi"
+
+/ {
+	model = "AVM FRITZ!Box 7590 AX";
+	compatible = "avm,fritz7590ax", "intel,xrx500";
+
+	/*
+	 * The boot image carries a table of device-tree blobs keyed by
+	 * hardware sub-revision, and the boot loader looks the unit's value
+	 * up in it or takes a lower entry. Units report 1 or 2, so this
+	 * tree takes 0, the vendor's own key for this board.
+	 */
+	avm-hw-revision {
+		compatible = "avm,avm_hw_revision";
+		revision = "259";
+		subrevision = "0";
+	};
+
+	/*
+	 * The node covers the full 512 MiB DDR part at PHYS_OFFSET.
+	 *
+	 * Only the first half of it has an uncached alias. Above
+	 * PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M, the KSEG1 alias of a page is a register
+	 * address rather than DRAM. That bound has not moved. What moved is
+	 * where it is enforced. ZONE_DMA is sized to the window, and
+	 * dma-direct takes every uncached coherent allocation from there.
+	 * Holding the bound therefore no longer costs the board half its
+	 * memory. Cached access is good across the whole node, since KSEG0
+	 * covers all of it.
+	 *
+	 * The width is coupled to the boot container, and the coupling is
+	 * exact rather than stylistic. The full width is safe only while the
+	 * second CPU's boot loader stays unstarted. That firmware declares
+	 * 256 MiB and programs a deny-all fabric region over everything
+	 * above it. That region is precisely the half this node adds. The
+	 * first access to that half then faults or hangs. There is no boot
+	 * loader blob for this board. The way to keep that loader unstarted
+	 * is therefore to ship the kernel in a single-kernel container,
+	 * which is what the image recipe does.
+	 */
+	memory@20000000 {
+		device_type = "memory";
+		reg = <0x20000000 0x20000000>;
+	};
+
+	/*
+	 * These aliases name the boot, failsafe and upgrade lamps for
+	 * userspace. Without them, the boot-progress indication is silently
+	 * absent.
+	 */
+	aliases {
+		serial0 = &asc0;
+
+		led-boot = &led_power;
+		led-running = &led_power;
+		led-failsafe = &led_info_red;
+		led-upgrade = &led_info;
+		led-wifi = &led_wlan;
+	};
+
+	chosen {
+		stdout-path = "serial0:115200n8";
+		/*
+		 * This property is unused while MIPS_CMDLINE_BUILTIN_EXTEND
+		 * is selected. It is present so that a board that needs its
+		 * own arguments has somewhere to put them.
+		 */
+		bootargs = "";
+	};
+
+	/*
+	 * Any region below that a device reaches through the uncached alias
+	 * must stay under PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M. Above that address, the
+	 * alias reaches registers, not DRAM. The seed buffer at the end is
+	 * the one region that sits higher. It may, because it is no-map and
+	 * this kernel never reaches it at all.
+	 */
+	reserved-memory {
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <1>;
+		ranges;
+
+		/*
+		 * Crash persistence sits at the address the vendor's own
+		 * device tree uses for it. The vendor's node is named
+		 * ramoops@20500000, but its reg says 0x20100000. It takes
+		 * 1 MiB of the 4 MiB region below _text. The zone sizes fill
+		 * reg exactly, since fs/pstore/ram.c fails the whole probe if
+		 * they overflow it.
+		 */
+		ramoops@20100000 {
+			compatible = "ramoops";
+			reg = <0x20100000 0x100000>;
+			record-size = <0x4000>;
+			console-size = <0x80000>;
+			mem-type = <1>;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * This is the rest of the region that seale_avm.dtsi reserves.
+		 * The crash log above has taken its first megabyte.
+		 */
+		/delete-node/ avm-reserved@20100000;
+
+		avm-reserved@20200000 {
+			reg = <0x20200000 0x300000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * This region is owned by the second CPU's boot loader. The
+		 * vendor ships a device tree of its own for that core. The
+		 * region is no-map so that this kernel never maps another
+		 * CPU's memory.
+		 */
+		cpunet@2dfc0000 {
+			reg = <0x2dfc0000 0x40000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		bootcore@2e000000 {
+			reg = <0x2e000000 0x2000000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * This is a fixed-address seed buffer shared with the second
+		 * CPU. It is reserved no-map so that nothing here maps or
+		 * reads it.
+		 */
+		shm-rng-seed@3dfb0000 {
+			reg = <0x3dfb0000 0x1000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * The flash is a Toshiba BENAND part of 512 MiB with a 4 KiB page. Its
+ * geometry is self-identified. The partition map is the one the vendor boot
+ * loader installs, so a vendor recovery can hand the kernel back to the
+ * original firmware without remapping flash. It is not the FRITZ!Box 7590's
+ * map, so that board's images must never be written to this flash.
+ */
+&nand0 {
+	partitions {
+		compatible = "fixed-partitions";
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <1>;
+
+		/*
+		 * This partition holds the first-stage boot loader. It is
+		 * read-only, because overwriting it bricks the device past the
+		 * point of recovery without an external programmer.
+		 */
+		partition@0 {
+			label = "urlader";
+			reg = <0x0 0x100000>;
+			read-only;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * This is the AVM TFFS environment. It holds the only copy of
+		 * this unit's identity, its MAC addresses and serial number.
+		 * It is never written from Linux. Erasing it cannot be undone.
+		 */
+		partition@100000 {
+			label = "nand-tffs";
+			reg = <0x100000 0x800000>;
+			read-only;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * Two 8 MiB kernel slots follow, named by position. Which one
+		 * is active is boot-loader run-time state. The linux_fs_start
+		 * variable selects between them. No label may therefore claim
+		 * to be the active one.
+		 */
+		partition@900000 {
+			label = "kernel0";
+			reg = <0x900000 0x800000>;
+		};
+
+		partition@1100000 {
+			label = "kernel1";
+			reg = <0x1100000 0x800000>;
+		};
+
+		/* UBI holds rootfs and rootfs_data up to the 512 MiB mark. */
+		partition@1900000 {
+			label = "ubi";
+			reg = <0x1900000 0x1e700000>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * RC0 and RC1 carry two Intel/MaxLinear WAV600 Wi-Fi parts, PCI ID 8086:09d0.
+ * The WAV624 on RC0 serves 5 GHz and the WAV614 on RC1 serves 2.4 GHz. No
+ * in-tree driver supports them. RC2 carries a DSL part of the VRX618 family,
+ * PCI ID 8086:09aa, which has no in-tree driver either. Inbound byte
+ * swapping stays on for this part.
+ */
+&pcie2 {
+	status = "okay";
+	intel,inbound-swap = <1>;
+	intel,outbound-swap = <0>;
+	reset-gpios = <&gpio0 16 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+};
+
+/*
+ * Front-panel lamps.
+ *
+ * The shift-register controller they hang off is a GRX550 fact and lives in
+ * seale_avm_grx550.dtsi. What is board data is which of its eight pins are
+ * populated and what each one drives. Six of the seven declared here are
+ * visible lamps, and the seventh is a brightness carrier that is not.
+ *
+ * No shift clock rate is stated, which leaves the driver's 25 MHz default.
+ * That is the rate the vendor's GRX550 layer asks for, and only its layer for
+ * the FPGA-less sub-revision lowers it.
+ *
+ * The die layer's 250 kHz update rate selects a timer outside the SSO block,
+ * which nothing in this kernel programs. Unless that timer already runs, no
+ * output word ever reaches the shift register. A 10 Hz rate is the fastest
+ * that selects the SSO block's own divided clock instead.
+ */
+&ssogpio {
+	intel,sso-update-rate-hz = <10>;
+
+	/*
+	 * The child must be named "ssoled", because the driver matches on
+	 * the node name. The LEDs carry no label property, since colour and
+	 * function compose the ABI-visible LED name.
+	 */
+	ssoled {
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <0>;
+
+		/*
+		 * This is not a lamp but a brightness carrier driven at a
+		 * fixed duty cycle. A child with neither colour nor function
+		 * degrades to the name "lgm-sso::".
+		 */
+		led@0 {
+			reg = <0>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_WHITE>;
+			function = "ambient";
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 0 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		/* The two halves of the bicolour info lamp. */
+		led_info: led@2 {
+			reg = <2>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = "info";
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 2 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		led_info_red: led@3 {
+			reg = <3>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_RED>;
+			function = "info";
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 3 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		led_wlan: led@4 {
+			reg = <4>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = LED_FUNCTION_WLAN;
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 4 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		led@5 {
+			reg = <5>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = "fon";
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 5 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		led@6 {
+			reg = <6>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = "connect";
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 6 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * The lamp is lit as soon as the driver probes. It is already
+		 * on when the kernel starts, and blinking it off mid-boot would
+		 * read as a failed boot. The line is active low.
+		 */
+		led_power: led@7 {
+			reg = <7>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = LED_FUNCTION_POWER;
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 7 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+			default-state = "on";
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/* The keys are polled, because the GPIO banks publish no irq domain. */
+/ {
+	keys {
+		compatible = "gpio-keys-polled";
+		poll-interval = <100>;
+
+		button-dect {
+			label = "dect";
+			linux,code = <KEY_PHONE>;
+			gpios = <&gpio1 0 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		};
+
+		button-wlan {
+			label = "wlan";
+			linux,code = <KEY_RFKILL>;
+			gpios = <&gpio0 2 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		};
+
+		button-connect {
+			label = "connect";
+			linux,code = <KEY_WPS_BUTTON>;
+			gpios = <&gpio0 1 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * The master LED enable is a plain GPIO rather than an SSO pin. Nothing in
+ * this tree consumes it, which is why it is hogged. The whole panel is dark
+ * while it is low.
+ */
+&gpio1 {
+	led-enable-hog {
+		gpio-hog;
+		gpios = <11 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		output-high;
+		line-name = "led-enable";
+	};
+};
